he Times (U.K.) and the bestselling author of The Spy and the Traitor, Operation Mincemeat, and Rogue Heroes, among many others. ... Publisher  | National Geographic Books, 2015\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Spy Among Friends: Kim Philby and the Great Betrayal - Ben Macintyre - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/A_Spy_Among_Friends.html?id=wIzIAgAAQBAJ)\nciteturn54search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; Ben Macintyre Crown, Jul 29, 2014 - History - 384 pages NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • The epic true story of Kim Philby, the Cold War’s most infamous spy, from the “master storyteller” (San Francisco Chronicle) and author of Prisoners of the Castle. ... Those closest to him—like his fellow MI6 officer and best friend since childhood, Nicholas Elliot, and the CIA’s head of counterintelligence, James Jesus Angleton—knew him as a loyal confidant and an unshakeable patriot. ... In A Spy Among Friends, Ben Macintyre expertly weaves the heart-pounding tale of how Philby almost got away with it all—and what happened when he was finally unmasked. ... Publisher  | Crown, 2014\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCOLD WAR BOOKS, DVDS, and RELATED ITEMS (https://coldwar.org/uploads/documents/SPRING2015.pdf)\nciteturn54search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.5 years ago; Book cover image for *A Spy Among Friends* showing a black-and-white profile of Kim Philby on the left and a red cover with large yellow title text “A SPY AMONG FRIENDS,” subtitle “KIM PHILBY AND THE GREAT BETRAYAL,” author name “BEN MACINTYRE,” and a circular “NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER” badge near the lower left. ... Every word Elliott breathed to Philby was transmitted back to Moscow—and not just Elliott’s words, for in America, Philby had made another powerful friend: James Jesus Angleton, the crafty, paranoid head of CIA counterintelligence.\n-----------------