ki/Guy_Burgess)\nciteturn54search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; John Murray. ... * Macintyre, Ben.A Spy Among Friends: Philby and the Great Betrayal. ... Guy Burgess: The Spy Who Knew Everyone.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nView PDF (https://www.itv.com/presscentre/print/pdf/node/7428)\nciteturn54search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Hotly anticipated cold war drama A Spy Among Friends is a series based on the New York Times best-selling book written by Ben Macintyre.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews (https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/military-review/Archives/English/MilitaryReview_20141231_art021.pdf)\nciteturn54search20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Crawled: 12.0 years ago; Ben Macintyre’s book *A Spy Among Friends* recounts the story of Kim Philby, a British MI6 (Military Intelligence, Section 6) officer and a member of the Cambridge Five, one of the most infamous Soviet spy rings. ... It is in Beirut where Elliott eventually accepted that his friend was a fraud and confronted him.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Spy Among Friends Q&A (https://www.itv.com/presscentre/presscentre/print/pdf/node/7428)\nciteturn54search21 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; A Spy Among Friends Q&A ... New York Times best-selling book written by Ben Macintyre.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLoyalty and affection is threatened in 'A Spy Among Friends' (https://apnews.com/article/d125992f75fcf41b01fb84d7f9b11f48)\nciteturn54news36 [wordlim: 100] Published: 3.6 years ago; Loyalty and affection is threatened in 'A Spy Among Friends' ... The series is adapted from a book by Ben Macintyre, who also wrote about the origins of Britain's elite Special Air Service which was recently turned into the series \"Rogue Heroes.\" ... In one scene, Elliott secretly weeps about the loss of his friend amid rolliking laughter from among the audience of a stage comedy.\nLoyalty and affection is threatened in 'A Spy Among Friends'\nDate: Fri Mar 10 16:19:27 2023 UTC\nLocation: New York, New York, United States\n\nNEW YORK (AP) - A main character in \"A Spy Among Friends\" is one of the Soviet Union's most notorious double agents. But you don't need to know anything about him - or really about spying in general - to enjoy the show.\nThe MGM+ series starring Damian Lewis and Guy Pearce dramatizes the true story of two British spies and lifelong friends, Nicholas Elliott (Lewis) and Kim Philby (Pearce.) The latter became a notorious British defector. But it's the personal betrayal that the creators hope to explore, not stolen microfilm or dead drops.\n\"We're not principally telling a spy story. We're telling a story about friendship. This is a series about friendship among people who happen to be in the espionage business,\" says director Nick Murphy.\nCreator and executive producer Alexander Cary and Murphy use 12 hours to unspool this epic betrayal over several decades, moving back and forth in time to capture past conversations and memories.\n\"She is representative of the next generation and a different type of person entirely - a woman and not from the social militia these guys come from,\" says Lewis. \"She is really there to illustrate that it's time for a change.\"\nThe series is adapted from a book by Ben Macintyre, who also wrote about the origins of Britain's elite Special Air Service which was recently turned into the series \"Rogue Heroes.\"\nUnlike that kinetic series, \"A Spy Among Friends\" is more measured, though no less gripping. It's a series awash in fedoras, clunky telephones and many, many glasses of whiskey. There is pelting rain, the crisp burn of cigarette paper and cups of tea rattling.\nBecause it deals with people hiding their true identity, small gestures like a raised eyebrow can telegraph what a character is really thinking. Feelings leak out, not gush. In one scene, Elliott secretly weeps about the loss of his friend amid rolliking laughter from among the audience of a stage comedy.\nPhilby's betrayal cut deep in Britain's psyche, more perhaps than American counterparts like Aldrich Ames or Robert Hanssen. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Spy Who Ruled Summer (https://time.com/2926409/the-spy-who-ruled-summer/)\nciteturn54news37 [wordlim: 100] Published: 12.3 years ago; That, by his own account, was the state of mind of MI6 officer David Cornwell, a.k.a.John le Carré, when he wrote The Spy Who Came In From the Cold, a book that dominated the summer of 1964 and shaped every depiction of espionage that followed. ... MI5 alum to author in Le Carré modeBEN MACINTYRE\n"
